In our interpretation, yes: TenneT TSO B.V. could apply on its own for charging stations at its own sites for its own vehicles or staff.
The rules let any company registered with the Chamber of Commerce with an establishment in the Netherlands apply. TenneT TSO B.V. is a private-law company with economic activity (electricity transmission) and is not one of the excluded public-law bodies, although it is state-owned; the rules do not exclude state-owned companies. Conditions that would apply to TenneT:
- the stations are on land TenneT owns or rents, such as an office, depot or substation site, and are not open to the public at all times;
- for a subsidy of EUR 25,000 or more, TenneT must apply before it orders the work, with an unsigned quote;
- the subsidy per location must be at least EUR 2,500.
RVO has not ruled on TenneT's eligibility; TenneT could confirm it with RVO, for example in the advice talk RVO offers.
